  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a chilly start with high pressure nearly overhead, the\nhigh will then shift east of the region this afternoon, allowing\nfor a light south wind to develop. Mainly sunny skies this\nmorning will give way to some high and mid level clouds this\nafternoon as mid level warm advection increases. Clouds will be\nmore prevalent north of I-90 later this afternoon. High temps\nshould reach the upper 60s to lower 70s in most valley areas.\n\nAn approaching cold front from the northwest will bring more\nclouds to the region late tonight into Wednesday, and there\ncould be just enough forcing for some isolated showers,\nespecially near and south of I-90. Temps should warm into the\n70s for most areas, possibly reaching around 80 across portions\nof the mid Hudson Valley.\n\nThe frontal system is expected to stall/wash out near or south\nof I-90 Wednesday night, as a wave of low pressure tracks along\nthe remnant boundary on Thursday. This wave should bring a\nbetter chance for showers and a few thunderstorms Thursday into\nThursday night. PWAT's increase to 1.50-1.75\" along with some\nweak, mainly elevated instability could allow for some embedded\nthunderstorms with heavier downpours during this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned frontal boundary should settle south of the\nregion Friday through early Saturday as high pressure slides\nnorth of the region, allowing for a period of dry weather with \nbelow normal temperatures. The front may then return northward \nahead of an approaching low pressure system from the Great Lakes\nregion, bringing showers back into the region Saturday \nafternoon or night and continuing into a portion of Sunday.\nTiming of these showers will be better refined as we get a bit\ncloser to this time range.",
